# Copy to .env.local and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Firebase web config. Public values — a web API key identifies the project
# rather than authenticating anyone, and it ships in the client bundle by
# design. Security comes from Firestore Security Rules and App Check.
#
# Find these at: Firebase console → Project settings → General → Your apps.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* is inlined at BUILD time, so these must be present when
# `next build` runs — not only at runtime.

NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_DATABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# Optional. Omit to disable Analytics entirely.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server credentials — a real secret, unlike everything above.
#
# Service account JSON, base64-encoded onto one line. The Admin SDK bypasses
# Security Rules, so this key can read and write every document in the project.
# Never expose it to the browser; lib/firebase/admin.ts imports `server-only`
# so that mistake fails the build.
#
# Firebase console → Project settings → Service accounts → Generate new
# private key. Then, on Windows:
#
#   [Convert]::ToBase64String([IO.File]::ReadAllBytes("service-account.json"))
#
# or elsewhere:
#
#   base64 -w0 service-account.json
#
# Leave blank when running on Google infrastructure — Application Default
# Credentials are picked up automatically.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Workload Identity Federation — server credentials with no key to leak.
#
# The preferred way to authenticate the Admin SDK, and the only one available
# under the `iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation` org policy, which forbids
# downloading the key above at all.
#
# Vercel issues each deployment a short-lived OIDC token — VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN,
# injected at runtime, not something to set by hand. Google exchanges it for an
# access token impersonating a service account, so nothing long-lived exists to
# be copied. Set both variables below and leave FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY
# blank; where a key is present it wins, so this is never a silent override.
#
# The audience is the pool provider's full resource name, one line, no scheme:
#
#   //iam.googleapis.com/projects/<project number>/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/<pool>/providers/<provider>
#
# DEPLOYMENT.md → "Workload Identity Federation" has the commands that create
# the pool and let the service account be impersonated through it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_AUDIENCE=
GCP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Razorpay — subscription payments.
#
# The key id is public: it ships in the checkout modal by design. The secret is
# not — it signs and verifies payments, so anything holding it can confirm a
# subscription. Server-only; lib/payments/razorpay.ts imports `server-only`.
#
# Test keys start rzp_test_ and move no real money. Live keys start rzp_live_.
# Find both at: Razorpay dashboard → Account & Settings → API Keys.
#
# Leave blank to run without card payment: checkout answers 503 and the console
# falls back to a bank-transfer reference rather than breaking.

RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=

# Razorpay dashboard → Settings → Webhooks. Point it at
#   https://<your-domain>/api/webhooks/razorpay
# subscribed to `payment.captured`, and paste the secret it gives you here.
#
# Without this the webhook refuses everything, and a payment whose browser
# never came back is never applied. It is not optional in production.
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Bypass payment while the rest of a flow is being built and tested.
#
#   PAYMENTS_BYPASS=true
#
# Choosing a plan then activates the subscription immediately and charges
# nothing. Every record it writes is stamped paymentMethod: "bypass", so the
# fakes can be found and cleared in one query before going live:
#
#   subscription.paymentMethod == "bypass"
#
# Two safeguards, because this is exactly the flag somebody forgets:
#   - it is ignored entirely if RAZORPAY_KEY_ID is an rzp_live_ key;
#   - every console that can create a subscription shows a banner while it is on.
#
# Leave it unset everywhere except the environment you are testing in.
PAYMENTS_BYPASS=

# Web Push, for phone notifications. Firebase console → Project settings →
# Cloud Messaging → Web configuration → Web Push certificates → generate a key
# pair, then paste the public key here. Public by design, like the other
# NEXT_PUBLIC values: it identifies the project to the browser's push service
# and authenticates nothing. Without it the "Notify me on this device" button
# says the deployment is not set up rather than failing when pressed.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_VAPID_KEY=

# The coverage map on the landing page.
#
# A Google Maps JavaScript API key, from https://console.cloud.google.com —
# create a project, enable "Maps JavaScript API", then Credentials -> API key.
# Google requires a billing account with a card on it before the API will
# answer, even inside the free allowance.
#
# Cost: the Dynamic Maps SKU gives 10,000 free map loads a month, then $7.00
# per 1,000. A load is only counted when somebody actually scrolls to the
# coverage section, because that is when the script is fetched — see
# components/marketing/coverage-map.tsx.
#
# Restrict the key to your domains (Credentials -> Application restrictions ->
# HTTP referrers) before it goes anywhere public. It ships to the browser by
# design, so the referrer allow-list is the only thing standing between it and
# somebody else's bill.
#
# The loader always sends region=IN. That is what decides which country's
# borders are drawn, and Google's localisation guide makes complying with local
# law the caller's responsibility — do not remove it.
#
# Leave it unset and the section renders the village cards alone, with no map
# and no map script downloaded at all.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# The renewal-reminder job.
#
# `/api/cron/subscription-reminders` runs on the schedule in vercel.json and is
# guarded by this shared secret — a cron has no user to be. Unset, the endpoint
# returns 503 rather than running: an open one would let a stranger message
# every subscriber on the platform in our name.
#
# Vercel sends it automatically as `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`.
CRON_SECRET=

# Channels for those reminders beyond in-app and push.
#
# All three are inert until both variables of a pair are set, and the job
# reports them as unconfigured rather than pretending to have sent. Each also
# needs paperwork that cannot be done in code: an Indian SMS sender needs the
# template registered on DLT, WhatsApp needs it approved in the Business
# account, and email needs SPF and DKIM on the sending domain.
SMS_PROVIDER_KEY=
SMS_SENDER_ID=
WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
WHATSAPP_PHONE_ID=
EMAIL_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RazorpayX — penny-drop bank verification.
#
# NOT the same account as RAZORPAY_KEY_ID above. That is the payment gateway,
# used for subscriptions; this is RazorpayX, Razorpay's business-banking
# product, with its own onboarding, its own dashboard and its own key pair.
# Reusing the gateway key here fails with an authentication error that reads
# like a typo.
#
# What it buys: adding a bank account on the profile page can be proved
# instantly — a rupee is sent to the account and the bank returns the name it
# holds, which is compared against the name on the account record.
#
# Three constraints worth knowing before you spend a day on this:
#
#   - There is NO TEST MODE. Account validation is live-only, so it cannot be
#     exercised on rzp_test_ keys or covered by CI. Everything decidable is
#     decided in lib/domain/bank-accounts.ts and lib/domain/name-match.ts,
#     which are tested; this integration is kept as thin as possible.
#   - RazorpayX requires the calling IPs to be ALLOWLISTED. Serverless
#     functions do not have stable egress addresses on every plan, and a call
#     from an unlisted address is refused in a way that looks like bad
#     credentials.
#   - Each attempt COSTS MONEY. MAX_VERIFY_ATTEMPTS caps it at three per
#     account, which is a spend limit as much as a sanity limit.
#
# Leave these unset and the bank page still works: accounts can be added and
# removed, verification is not offered, and the page says operations will check
# by hand rather than showing a button that always fails.
#
# RAZORPAYX_ACCOUNT_NUMBER is the RazorpayX account the rupee is debited from —
# the one shown on the RazorpayX dashboard, not a customer's account.
RAZORPAYX_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAYX_KEY_SECRET=
RAZORPAYX_ACCOUNT_NUMBER=

# RazorpayX dashboard → Settings → Webhooks. Point it at
#   https://<your-domain>/api/webhooks/razorpayx
# subscribed to the fund_account.validation events, and paste the secret here.
#
# A separate secret from RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET, and a separate endpoint:
# two products, two dashboards, two signing keys. Without this the validation
# result never lands and an account sits on "Checking…" until the page polls it
# — which works, but only while somebody is watching.
RAZORPAYX_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The site's own public address, e.g. https://pasumaitrade.com
#
# Read by lib/site-url.ts. It decides the canonical URL on every public page,
# the absolute URLs in sitemap.xml and robots.txt, and the Open Graph image
# URL a crawler or a chat app fetches — so if it is wrong, those point
# somewhere else, and if it is unset outside Vercel they fall back to
# http://localhost:3000.
#
# Optional on Vercel, which supplies VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL as the next
# preference. Set it explicitly anyway once a custom domain is live: it is the
# only source that cannot disagree with the domain people actually visit.

NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=
